fyi, we have a script on NAO that can recreate the record file from
the logfile. It is pretty trivial to to, so this might save you all of
15 minutes of effort. There are probably more efficient ways to do
this, and my perl-fu is weak, but this gets the job done. This script
just sorts the logfile by high score. Just pipe it through a 'head'
command to get the number of entries you want output.

For instance:

# cat logfile > ./makerecord.pl | head -n 2000

will rebuild the NAO record file from the logfile.

+++++++++++++ makerecord.pl

#!/usr/bin/perl

while(<STDIN>) {
	$line =3D $_;
	chomp($line);
	$line =3D~ /(\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+)
(\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+) (\S+),(\S+)/;
	$linearray[++$num] =3D $line;
	$namearray[$num] =3D $16;
	$typearray[$num] =3D $12;
	$score[$num] =3D $2 . ' ' . $num;
}

@sorted =3D sort {$a <=3D> $b} @score;

while($top =3D pop sorted) {
	$top =3D~ /(\S+) (\S+)/;
	$v =3D $1;
	$k =3D $2;
	$used{$namearray[$k].$typearray[$k]}++;
	if (($used{$namearray[$k].$typearray[$k]} <=3D 10) && ($v > 0)) {
		print $linearray[$k] . "\n";
	}
}



